---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Layout title="FAQ" description="Answers to common Keyboard Helper setup and troubleshooting questions.">
  <style>
    h1 { font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 8px; }
    .subtitle { color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 36px; font-size: 1rem; }
    .faq-list { display: grid; gap: 16px; }
    article {
      background: var(--bg2);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 22px;
    }
    h2 { color: var(--accent); font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 12px; }
    p, li { color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65; }
    p + p { margin-top: 10px; }
    ol { margin-top: 12px; padding-left: 22px; }
    li + li { margin-top: 6px; }
    code {
      background: var(--bg);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 4px;
      color: var(--accent);
      font-family: "Cascadia Code", "Fira Code", "Courier New", monospace;
      font-size: 0.88em;
      padding: 1px 5px;
    }
    .important {
      border-color: rgba(60, 201, 199, 0.4);
      box-shadow: inset 3px 0 0 var(--accent);
    }
  </style>

  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Quick answers for installing, configuring, and troubleshooting Keyboard Helper.</p>

  <section class="faq-list" aria-label="Frequently asked questions">
    <article class="important">
      <h2>Why are key presses not highlighted on macOS?</h2>
      <p>Keyboard Helper needs <strong>Input Monitoring</strong> to observe keys globally. Accessibility alone is not enough.</p>
      <p>If the permission switch is already on but highlighting still does not work, refresh the app entry instead of only toggling it:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Move the current <em>Keyboard Layout.app</em> to <code>/Applications</code>.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Input Monitoring</strong>.</li>
        <li>Remove any existing <em>Keyboard Layout</em> entry with the minus button.</li>
        <li>Add the current application bundle again and enable it.</li>
        <li>Quit Keyboard Helper from the tray menu, then launch it again.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>This can be necessary after rebuilding the app or switching to another downloaded copy, because macOS can retain permission state for the earlier bundle.</p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h2>Where did the window go after I closed it?</h2>
      <p>Closing the window hides Keyboard Helper to the system tray instead of quitting it. Use the tray icon to restore the overlay, or launch the app with <code>--toggle</code> if you have configured an external shortcut.</p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h2>How do I select a different layout or use my own?</h2>
      <p>Open <strong>Settings</strong> from the application menu, choose <strong>Add layout file…</strong>, and select your JSON file. You can then make it the startup layout and save. Direct <code>~/.keyri.json</code> editing remains available for advanced use; the <a href={`${base}/setup/`}>setup guide</a> includes examples.</p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h2>Does Keyboard Helper work only with split keyboards?</h2>
      <p>No. It includes QWERTY, QWERTZ, Mac, and Magic Keyboard layouts as well as Corne and Dactyl. The overlay highlights the key codes reported by the operating system, so it can also visualize compatible custom layouts.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</Layout>
